{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012wo2JiKiuk17jGUNBCdSDE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012zEJP4FVBmtsnTccn4BgSr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17916,"cache_read_input_tokens":19263,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17916,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcjRwnpoLXHM9pNf7QHJEG","session_id":"1b0edaaf-f3d3-4d02-90e4-a445b4dd87c2","uuid":"8d492e1a-08a0-4351-9ad1-aa554903eeea"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012zEJP4FVBmtsnTccn4BgSr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?\n5\t\n6\tWe find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration we test degrades performance catastrophically: fixed $\\dt{=}2$ collapses to $14\\%$ of baseline, fixed $\\dt{=}4$ never exceeds $19\\%$ of baseline, and a learned Gumbel-Softmax gate collapses to $11\\%$. Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \\citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \\emph{trained} $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor within the factorial design tested. This trained-and-exposed embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates---a failure mode we term \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). A $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold (35K+ window) achieves 7/8 CIVO classification ($n{=}8$, single environment), with one non-CIVO false positive---a failure mode analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional} but arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. Gradient decomposition reveals that 93\\% of temporal embedding gradients originate from observation decoders rather than the reward head (which contributes 0\\%), reframing the mechanism: CIVO is driven by observation reconstruction gradients rather than reward-specific pathways. A full-pathway gradient-stopping intervention that removes observation decoder gradients from the temporal embedding completely eliminates CIVO (eval 915.7, NVE $1.00\\times$---indistinguishable from the healthy baseline), confirming the gradient-level mechanism.\n7\t\n8\tThree further findings complicate the picture: (1)~the same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse, transient CIVO with self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---across seeds of identical configurations; (2)~restricting imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding does not prevent collapse, with one of two seeds failing via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (evaluation-driven, increasing entropy); and (3)~the contrast with TAWM identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.\n9\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TcjRwnpoLXHM9pNf7QHJEG","session_id":"1b0edaaf-f3d3-4d02-90e4-a445b4dd87c2","uuid":"dac9e6ce-5d6f-4646-9512-bbaf5851062c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:27:30.029Z"}
