{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015SbyN9CJYQk7PF6NWN65xx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VJLQbAbT8Z4xg6Vn6tBSZW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T133210/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":402,"cache_read_input_tokens":49613,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":402,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhLTKsURT834Q9J999V9EY","session_id":"1b0edaaf-f3d3-4d02-90e4-a445b4dd87c2","uuid":"03e40b3c-7ba9-4609-8943-6967b91088f8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VJLQbAbT8Z4xg6Vn6tBSZW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{array}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcolumntype{C}{>{\\centering\\arraybackslash}X}\n15\t\\newcolumntype{R}{>{\\raggedleft\\arraybackslash}X}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate\\\\World-Model Imagination: A Diagnostic Analysis}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{\n20\tAnonymous Authors\n21\t}\n22\t\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t%% ============================================================\n30\t%% ABSTRACT\n31\t%% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tTemporal abstraction---imagining at variable time scales---is a natural capability of learned world models, yet prior attempts to exploit it in DreamerV3 consistently degrade performance.\n34\tWe present a systematic factorial diagnosis identifying the root cause: a \\emph{trained dt-embedding} that conditions the critic on temporal-skip information triggers \\textbf{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)}, a positive-feedback loop in which the critic exploits precise but inaccurate dt-conditioned predictions from the RSSM.\n35\tUsing a normalized value-to-eval (NVE) diagnostic, we show that a 1.5$\\times$ threshold at 35K+ steps perfectly separates pathological from healthy runs (16/16 accuracy across 4 environments: Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop).\n36\tGradient decomposition reveals that 93\\% of the dt-embedding gradient originates from observation decoders, not the reward head, pinpointing the architectural vulnerability.\n37\tRestricting imagination to dt$=$1 eliminates the primary CIVO pathway but exposes a secondary eval-driven instability (Pathway B, 1/4 seeds), demonstrating that surface-level mitigations are insufficient.\n38\tNotably, TAWM (Nhu et al., 2025) succeeds with dt-conditioning under MPC, confirming that the vulnerability is specific to imagination-based actor-critic architectures.\n39\tOur findings provide actionable guidance: do not train dt-embeddings for temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic systems without safeguards against the observation-decoder gradient pathway.\n40\t\\end{abstract}\n41\t\n42\t%% ============================================================\n43\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n44\t%% ============================================================\n45\t\\section{Introduction}\n46\t\n47\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and plan at multiple time scales---has long been a goal of reinforcement learning research, from the options framework \\citep{sutton1999options} to macro-actions and action repetition \\citep{lakshminarayanan2017dynamic}.\n48\tWorld-model reinforcement learning, exemplified by the Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dreamer,hafner2023dreamerv3}, offers a particularly natural testbed: one can modify the imagination horizon's temporal granularity without changing environment interaction, enabling variable-rate ``mental simulation.''\n49\t\n50\tDespite this appeal, our systematic exploration of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 reveals a striking negative result: \\emph{every} configuration that introduces dt$>$1 imagination degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt$=$1 baseline.\n51\tFixed dt$=$2 imagination peaks at 485 (51\\% of baseline 950) before collapsing; dt$=$4 reaches only 177 (19\\% of baseline); learned gating peaks at 541 then collapses to 99.\n52\t\n53\tThis paper asks: \\emph{why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?}\n54\t\n55\tThrough a controlled factorial experiment design, we identify the root cause as \\textbf{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)}: when the dt-embedding is \\emph{trained} (receives gradient from the world model's observation decoders), it enables the critic to make precise but systematically biased dt-specific value predictions.\n56\tThe RSSM's prediction fidelity degrades at dt$>$1 (reward gap 2.78$\\times$ for trained dt-embedding vs.\\ 1.01$\\times$ without), but the trained embedding creates the illusion of reliable conditioning, triggering a positive-feedback loop: overestimated values $\\to$ actor exploitation $\\to$ policy degradation $\\to$ further overestimation.\n57\t\n58\tOur contributions are:\n59\t\\begin{enumerate}\n60\t    \\item A \\textbf{complete causal chain} from trained dt-embedding $\\to$ observation-decoder gradient amplification $\\to$ CIVO $\\to$ performance collapse, validated across 4 DMC environments with 16/16 NVE threshold accuracy.\n61\t    \\item A \\textbf{gradient decomposition} showing 93\\% of dt-embedding gradient comes from observation decoders (not reward), identifying the precise architectural vulnerability and explaining why reward-head interventions are ineffective.\n62\t    \\item A \\textbf{dual-pathway characterization}: CIVO (Pathway A, value-driven, entropy$\\downarrow$) vs.\\ eval-driven instability (Pathway B, entropy$\\uparrow$), showing that trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms.\n63\t    \\item A \\textbf{cross-architecture contrast}: dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based actor-critic, localizing the vulnerability to the imagination training loop.\n64\t\\end{enumerate}\n65\t\n66\t%% ============================================================\n67\t%% 2. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n68\t%% ============================================================\n69\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n72\tThe options framework \\citep{sutton1999options} formalizes temporal abstraction through temporally extended actions.\n73\tFiGAR \\citep{sharma2017figar} learns action repetition counts.\n74\tAction repeat is widely used as a hyperparameter in continuous control \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}.\n75\tThese model-free approaches do not suffer from the world-model fidelity gap that drives CIVO.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n78\tThe Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dreamer,hafner2021dreamerv2,hafner2023dreamerv3} learns latent-dynamics models (RSSM) and trains actor-critic networks entirely in imagination.\n79\tIRIS \\citep{micheli2023iris} and TWM \\citep{robine2023twm} use transformer-based world models.\n80\tVariable-rate imagination has been explored informally but without systematic diagnosis of failure modes.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in world models.}\n83\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} successfully uses dt-conditioning in TD-MPC2, an MPC-based planner.\n84\tCritically, TAWM does \\emph{not} train actor-critic networks in imagination---it uses the world model for online planning.\n85\tOur finding that dt-conditioning fails specifically in imagination-based actor-critic training but succeeds in MPC is a key novelty.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n88\tModel exploitation and value overestimation are known challenges in model-based RL \\citep{janner2019mbpo}.\n89\tLu et al.\\ (2018) formalize ``delusional bias'' from policy-class constraints creating unrealizable Q-values.\n90\tCIVO is mechanistically distinct: it arises from \\emph{model imprecision amplified by learned conditioning}, not from policy-class constraints.\n91\tWe use the term CIVO to avoid confusion with Lu et al.'s delusional bias.\n92\t\n93\t%% ============================================================\n94\t%% 3. METHODOLOGY\n95\t%% ============================================================\n96\t\\section{Methodology}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Base Architecture}\n99\t\n100\tWe use DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} with its default RSSM world model, actor-critic imagination training, and symlog predictions.\n101\tThe environment interaction loop is unmodified; all changes occur within the imagination and world-model training pipelines.\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination}\n104\t\n105\tWe introduce a \\textbf{dt-embedding} $e_{\\text{dt}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ that conditions the RSSM transition model, reward head, and continuation head on the temporal skip $\\text{dt} \\in \\{1,2,4\\}$.\n106\t\\textbf{Temporal subsampling} constructs training sequences by skipping every other transition (dt$=$2) or every third (dt$=$4), with the RSSM learning to predict across the gap.\n107\tA \\textbf{learned gating network} $g_\\phi(s_t) \\in [0,1]$ selects dt per imagination step.\n108\t\n109\t\\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}\n110\t\n111\tWe design a controlled ablation isolating the factors contributing to failure:\n112\t\n113\t\\begin{itemize}\n114\t    \\item \\textbf{dt-embedding status}: trained (gradient ON, subsample ON), untrained (gradient ON, subsample OFF---never sees dt$>$1 data), frozen (gradient OFF, random init), absent (dim$=$0).\n115\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal subsampling}: ON (dt$>$1 transitions in training data) vs.\\ OFF.\n116\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination horizon}: H$=$8 vs.\\ H$=$15.\n117\t    \\item \\textbf{Imagination dt}: dt$=$1 only vs.\\ dt$=$2 vs.\\ learned gate.\n118\t\\end{itemize}\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Normalized Value-to-Eval ratio (NVE).}\n123\tWe define $\\text{NVE}(t) = \\frac{v(t)/e(t)}{v_{\\text{base}}(t)/e_{\\text{base}}(t)}$ where $v(t)$ is the mean predicted value and $e(t)$ is the evaluation return at step $t$.\n124\tNVE$>$1.0 indicates the value function is more optimistic than baseline calibration; NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ at $t \\geq$ 35K (when baseline v/e has converged past 0.24) serves as the pathological threshold.\n125\tThe 35K+ constraint prevents false positives from early-training noise when v/e denominators are small.\n126\t\n127\t\\paragraph{Actor entropy.}\n128\tPolicy entropy distinguishes CIVO (Pathway A: entropy decreases as actor becomes overconfidently deterministic) from eval-driven instability (Pathway B: entropy increases as policy destabilizes toward randomness).\n129\t\n130\t\\paragraph{dt-embedding cosine similarity.}\n131\t$\\cos(e_{\\text{dt}=1}, e_{\\text{dt}=2})$ tracks embedding divergence during training, providing a mechanistic indicator of when the critic gains dt-discriminative power.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Environments}\n134\t\n135\tWe evaluate across four DeepMind Control Suite environments spanning different observation dimensionalities and dynamics:\n136\tWalker Walk (24-dim), Cheetah Run (17-dim), Cartpole Swingup (5-dim), and Hopper Hop (15-dim).\n137\tCross-environment replication elevates findings from task-specific to architectural.\n138\t\n139\t%% ============================================================\n140\t%% 4. EXPERIMENTS AND RESULTS\n141\t%% ============================================================\n142\t\\section{Experiments and Results}\n143\t\n144\t\\subsection{All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade Performance (C1)}\n145\t\n146\tTable~\\ref{tab:c1} summarizes all dt$>$1 configurations tested in Walker Walk.\n147\tEvery configuration degrades relative to the dt$=$1 baseline ($\\sim$950 eval return), with no positive result for variable-rate imagination.\n148\t\n149\t\\begin{table}[h]\n150\t\\centering\n151\t\\caption{Variable-rate imagination consistently degrades performance in Walker Walk.}\n152\t\\label{tab:c1}\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\t\\textbf{Configuration} & \\textbf{Peak Eval} & \\textbf{\\% of Baseline} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tBaseline (dt$=$1) & 950 & 100\\% & Stable convergence \\\\\n158\tFixed dt$=$2 (H$=$15, trained) & 485 & 51\\% & Collapse to $\\sim$135 \\\\\n159\tFixed dt$=$4 & 177 & 19\\% & Severe degradation \\\\\n160\tLearned gate & 541 & 57\\% & Collapse to 99 \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\end{table}\n164\t\n165\t\\subsection{Trained dt-Embedding Triggers Pathological Value Overestimation (C2)}\n166\t\\label{sec:c2}\n167\t\n168\tThe critical ablation compares four dt-embedding configurations in Walker Walk (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation}).\n169\tOnly the trained dt-embedding produces NVE$>$1.5$\\times$; all other configurations remain below.\n170\t\n171\t\\begin{table}[h]\n172\t\\centering\n173\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 dt-embedding ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H$=$8). Only trained dt-embedding with active gradient flow produces pathological NVE.}\n174\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n175\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n176\t\\toprule\n177\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{dt\\_emb Status} & \\textbf{NVE Peak (35K+)} & \\textbf{Collapse} & \\textbf{Failure Mode} \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\t008d & Trained (grad ON) & 4.12$\\times$ & Yes (1/2) & CIVO \\\\\n180\t008f & Absent (dim$=$0) & 1.26$\\times$ & No & Reward degradation \\\\\n181\t015b & Frozen (grad OFF) & 0.47$\\times$ & No & Gradient starvation \\\\\n182\t008c & Untrained (sub OFF) & 0.90$\\times$ & No & Learning signal reduction \\\\\n183\t\\bottomrule\n184\t\\end{tabular}\n185\t\\end{table}\n186\t\n187\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed NVE trajectories.}\n188\tTable~\\ref{tab:nve_seeds} shows the NVE trajectory for the trained dt-embedding configuration (008d) across two seeds.\n189\tBoth seeds exceed 1.5$\\times$ NVE at 35K+, but outcomes diverge: seed 1 sustains overestimation and collapses (eval 131@105K), while seed 2 self-corrects (eval 268@105K).\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[h]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\caption{NVE trajectory comparison for trained dt-embedding (008d), Walker Walk. Both seeds cross the 1.5$\\times$ threshold; collapse is stochastic.}\n194\t\\label{tab:nve_seeds}\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Seed 1 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 1 Eval} & \\textbf{Seed 2 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 2 Eval} \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\t25K & 1.78 & 167 & 2.05 & 141 \\\\\n200\t35K & 1.71 & 146 & 1.24 & 164 \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhLTKsURT834Q9J999V9EY","session_id":"1b0edaaf-f3d3-4d02-90e4-a445b4dd87c2","uuid":"86c06f76-b2b1-43b9-922b-f46ca87d2ed3","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:31:03.117Z"}
