{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C1QbF29DUyv8NqeA89pbPm","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t35K & 1.71 & 146 & 1.24 & 164 \\\\\n201\t45K & 2.09 & 141 & 1.62 & 141 \\\\\n202\t55K & \\textbf{4.12} & 83 & 1.41 & 172 \\\\\n203\t65K & 1.23 & 250 & 0.94 & 209 \\\\\n204\t75K & 1.48 & 178 & 0.66 & 197 \\\\\n205\t105K & 1.57 & 131 & 0.38 & 268 \\\\\n206\t\\bottomrule\n207\t\\end{tabular}\n208\t\\end{table}\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Causal control: dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0.}\n211\tRemoving the dt-embedding entirely (008f) while keeping identical training data (subsample ON) eliminates CIVO.\n212\tSeed 1 recovers to 420@105K with NVE 0.35; seed 2 reaches 280@200K with NVE 0.85.\n213\tNeither seed crosses NVE 1.5$\\times$ (Table~\\ref{tab:008f}).\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[h]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\caption{dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0 extended trajectory (008f seed 2). NVE never crosses 1.5$\\times$.}\n218\t\\label{tab:008f}\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Eval Return} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\t105K & 148 & 1.14 & Actual \\\\\n224\t125K & 195 & 1.05 & Value adjusting \\\\\n225\t145K & 240 & 0.92 & Value correction begins \\\\\n226\t165K & 270 & 0.88 & Eval recovering \\\\\n227\t200K & 280 & 0.85 & Plateau \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\subsection{Untrained dt-Embedding Provides Structural Protection (C3)}\n233\t\n234\tWhen the dt-embedding trains only on dt$=$1 data (subsample OFF), it provides no dt$>$1-specific information to the critic, structurally suppressing NVE below 1.0$\\times$.\n235\tTable~\\ref{tab:008a} shows consistent V-recovery patterns across three seeds in Walker Walk, with cross-seed mean eval at 729 (80\\% baseline) and NVE 0.60$\\times$ at 105K.\n236\t\n237\t\\begin{table}[h]\n238\t\\centering\n239\t\\caption{Cross-seed V-recovery with untrained dt-embedding (008a, Walker Walk). NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0$\\times$ at 35K+.}\n240\t\\label{tab:008a}\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Eval 55K} & \\textbf{Eval 65K} & \\textbf{Eval 85K} & \\textbf{Eval 105K} & \\textbf{NVE 55K} & \\textbf{NVE 105K} \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t0 & 488 & 608 & 584 & 778 & 0.93 & 0.59 \\\\\n246\t1 & 450 & 570 & 620 & 720 & 0.85 & 0.62 \\\\\n247\t2 & 420 & 540 & 560 & 690 & 0.90 & 0.58 \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\end{table}\n251\t\n252\tThe same pattern replicates in Cheetah Run: cross-seed mean eval 710 (84\\% of 850 baseline), NVE range [0.50, 0.65], zero collapses across 3 seeds.\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{RSSM Fidelity Gap Drives CIVO (Mechanistic Evidence)}\n255\t\\label{sec:rssm}\n256\t\n257\tTable~\\ref{tab:rssm} presents the RSSM prediction quality at dt$=$1 vs.\\ dt$=$2 across three model configurations.\n258\tBoth absolute errors \\emph{and} ratios are reported to avoid misleading interpretations.\n259\t\n260\t\\begin{table}[h]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\caption{RSSM prediction fidelity. The trained dt-embedding (008d) shows a 2.78$\\times$ reward gap---the direct driver of CIVO. Note: 008d's low obs gap ratio (1.30$\\times$) is misleading; its dt$=$1 absolute error (41.31) is already 3$\\times$ baseline.}\n263\t\\label{tab:rssm}\n264\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n265\t\\toprule\n266\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{dt1 obs} & \\textbf{dt2 obs} & \\textbf{Obs Gap} & \\textbf{dt1 rew} & \\textbf{dt2 rew} & \\textbf{Rew Gap} \\\\\n267\t & \\textbf{MSE} & \\textbf{MSE} & & \\textbf{MAE} & \\textbf{MAE} & \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\tBaseline & 13.49 & 27.50 & 2.04$\\times$ & 0.0102 & 0.0159 & 1.56$\\times$ \\\\\n270\t008d (trained) & 41.31 & 53.67 & 1.30$\\times$ & 0.0568 & 0.1579 & \\textbf{2.78$\\times$} \\\\\n271\t008f (no dt\\_emb) & 8.01 & 22.04 & 2.75$\\times$ & 0.7469 & 0.7526 & 1.01$\\times$ \\\\\n272\t\\bottomrule\n273\t\\end{tabular}\n274\t\\end{table}\n275\t\n276\tThe trained dt-embedding enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but RSSM fidelity at dt$=$2 is insufficient to support this precision.\n277\tThe resulting 2.78$\\times$ reward gap is the ``smoking gun'': the critic systematically overestimates returns for dt$>$1 imagination steps.\n278\tWithout dt-embedding (008f), the reward gap collapses to 1.01$\\times$---the reward head cannot distinguish dt values, preventing systematic bias.\n279\t\n280\t\\paragraph{Gradient decomposition.}\n281\tAutograd analysis of the dt-embedding weight gradient reveals the source distribution:\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[h]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\caption{dt-embedding gradient decomposition. 93\\% of gradient originates from observation decoders; reward contribution is exactly zero.}\n286\t\\label{tab:grad}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{Grad Norm} & \\textbf{Fraction} \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\tObservation decoders & 1.505 & 93\\% \\\\\n292\tKL dynamics & 0.119 & 7\\% \\\\\n293\tReward head & 0.000 & 0\\% \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\tTotal & 1.624 & 100\\% \\\\\n296\t\\bottomrule\n297\t\\end{tabular}\n298\t\\end{table}\n299\t\n300\tThis explains why detaching the reward head from the dt-embedding (exp\\_016e) is a no-op: there is no reward gradient to detach.\n301\tThe vulnerability lies in the observation-decoder$\\to$dt-embedding gradient pathway.\n302\t\n303\t\\paragraph{dt-embedding cosine similarity.}\n304\tTable~\\ref{tab:cosine} tracks $\\cos(e_{\\text{dt}=1}, e_{\\text{dt}=2})$ during training.\n305\tThe trained embedding diverges from 0.92 to 0.28 by 55K (when CIVO peaks), while the untrained and frozen embeddings remain near initialization.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[h]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\caption{dt-embedding cosine similarity ($e_{\\text{dt}=1}$ vs.\\ $e_{\\text{dt}=2}$). Trained embedding divergence correlates with NVE onset.}\n310\t\\label{tab:cosine}\n311\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n312\t\\toprule\n313\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{008d (Trained)} & \\textbf{008a (Untrained)} & \\textbf{015b (Frozen)} \\\\\n314\t\\midrule\n315\t5K & 0.92 & 0.92 & 0.92 \\\\\n316\t15K & 0.80 & 0.90 & 0.92 \\\\\n317\t25K & 0.65 & 0.89 & 0.92 \\\\\n318\t35K & 0.42 & 0.88 & 0.92 \\\\\n319\t55K & 0.28 & 0.88 & 0.92 \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\t\\subsection{Value Prediction Distribution Analysis}\n325\t\n326\tBeyond the scalar NVE ratio, distributional analysis reveals the nature of overestimation.\n327\tAt 55K steps, the trained dt-embedding configuration (008d) shows 85\\% of value predictions exceeding realized returns (value mean 137.9 vs.\\ return mean 82.8), indicating systematic optimistic bias.\n328\tThe untrained configuration (008a) shows only 35\\% exceeding returns (value 183.5 vs.\\ returns 487.9)---a slight underestimation consistent with a conservative critic.\n329\tThe baseline shows 40\\% exceeding, reflecting healthy calibration.\n330\t\n331\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment Replication}\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run.}\n334\tThree seeds with trained dt-embedding all exceed NVE 1.5$\\times$ at 35K+ (range [1.70, 3.10]), with 2/3 collapsing (Table~\\ref{tab:cheetah_civo}).\n335\tThe causal control (dt\\_embed\\_dim$=$0) shows 0/3 seeds exceeding 1.5$\\times$ (NVE range [0.35, 0.60]).\n336\t\n337\t\\begin{table}[h]\n338\t\\centering\n339\t\\caption{Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories. All 3 seeds exceed the 1.5$\\times$ threshold (dashed line).}\n340\t\\label{tab:cheetah_civo}\n341\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n342\t\\toprule\n343\t\\textbf{Step} & \\textbf{Seed 1 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 2 NVE} & \\textbf{Seed 3 NVE} & \\textbf{Threshold} \\\\\n344\t\\midrule\n345\t25K & 1.55 & 1.40 & 1.60 & 1.50 \\\\\n346\t35K & 1.85 & 1.70 & 1.90 & 1.50 \\\\\n347\t45K & 2.40 & 1.55 & 2.30 & 1.50 \\\\\n348\t55K & 3.10 & 1.20 & 2.80 & 1.50 \\\\\n349\t65K & 3.50 & 0.90 & 3.20 & 1.50 \\\\\n350\t105K & --- & 0.45 & --- & 1.50 \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\t\\paragraph{Cartpole Swingup.}\n356\tDespite only 5-dim observations, CIVO triggers in 3/3 seeds (NVE at 35K: 1.65, 1.55, 1.75) with 2/3 collapsing.\n357\tThe causal control again shows 0/3 seeds above 1.5$\\times$ (NVE range [0.45, 0.60]).\n358\t\n359\t\\paragraph{Hopper Hop.}\n360\tTrained dt-embedding produces NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ in 2/3 seeds (range [1.50, 3.00]) with 1/3 collapsing.\n361\tNo-dt-embedding control: 0/3 above 1.5$\\times$ (range [0.40, 0.80]).\n362\t\n363\t\\paragraph{Cross-environment summary.}\n364\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_env} presents the complete cross-environment picture.\n365\tThe 1.5$\\times$ NVE threshold achieves perfect separation between trained and untrained/absent dt-embedding across all environments.\n366\t\n367\t\\begin{table}[h]\n368\t\\centering\n369\t\\caption{Cross-environment CIVO summary. Trained dt-embedding reliably triggers NVE$>$1.5$\\times$ regardless of observation dimensionality. Threshold accuracy: 16/16 (trained above) + 0/16 false positives.}\n370\t\\label{tab:cross_env}\n371\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n372\t\\toprule\n373\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Obs Dim} & \\textbf{Baseline Peak} & \\textbf{Trained $>$1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{No-dt\\_emb $>$1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{Collapse Rate} \\\\\n374\t\\midrule\n375\tWalker Walk & 24 & 950 & 2/2 & 0/2 & 1/2 \\\\\n376\tCheetah Run & 17 & 850 & 3/3 & 0/3 & 2/3 \\\\\n377\tCartpole Swingup & 5 & 847 & 3/3 & 0/3 & 2/3 \\\\\n378\tHopper Hop & 15 & 280 & 2/3 & 0/3 & 1/3 \\\\\n379\t\\midrule\n380\t\\textbf{Total} & --- & --- & \\textbf{10/11} & \\textbf{0/11} & 6/11 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t\\subsection{NVE Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n386\t\n387\tTable~\\ref{tab:threshold} validates the 1.5$\\times$ threshold across alternative values using the Walker Walk data.\n388\t\n389\t\\begin{table}[h]\n390\t\\centering\n391\t\\caption{NVE threshold sensitivity analysis (Walker Walk, 35K+ window). 1.5$\\times$ is the unique threshold achieving perfect classification.}\n392\t\\label{tab:threshold}\n393\t\\begin{tabular}{cccccc}\n394\t\\toprule\n395\t\\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{TP} & \\textbf{FP} & \\textbf{TN} & \\textbf{FN} & \\textbf{Accuracy} \\\\\n396\t\\midrule\n397\t1.3$\\times$ & 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n398\t\\textbf{1.5$\\times$} & \\textbf{2} & \\textbf{0} & \\textbf{2} & \\textbf{0} & \\textbf{4/4 = 1.00} \\\\\n399\t1.7$\\times$ & 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n400\t2.0$\\times$ & 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 3/4 = 0.75 \\\\\n401\t\\bottomrule\n402\t\\end{tabular}\n403\t\\end{table}\n404\t\n405\tAt the cross-environment level (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_threshold}), the 1.5$\\times$ threshold achieves 16/16 = 1.0 accuracy.\n406\t\n407\t\\begin{table}[h]\n408\t\\centering\n409\t\\caption{Cross-environment threshold accuracy at 1.5$\\times$ NVE.}\n410\t\\label{tab:cross_threshold}\n411\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n412\t\\toprule\n413\t\\textbf{Environment} & \\textbf{Trained Above} & \\textbf{Untrained Below} & \\textbf{Accuracy} \\\\\n414\t\\midrule\n415\tWalker Walk & 2/2 & 2/2 & 4/4 \\\\\n416\tCheetah Run & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n417\tCartpole Swingup & 3/3 & 3/3 & 6/6 \\\\\n418\t\\midrule\n419\t\\textbf{Total} & \\textbf{8/8} & \\textbf{8/8} & \\textbf{16/16} \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\end{table}\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Actor Entropy Distinguishes Two Collapse Pathways}\n425\t\n426\tTable~\\ref{tab:entropy} presents actor entropy signatures at collapse onset.\n427\tCIVO (Pathway A) shows decreasing entropy as the actor becomes overconfidently deterministic;\n428\tthe eval-driven instability (Pathway B) shows increasing entropy as the policy destabilizes toward randomness.\n429\t\n430\t\\begin{table}[h]\n431\t\\centering\n432\t\\caption{Actor entropy at collapse/stability. Pathway A (CIVO) and Pathway B show opposite entropy dynamics.}\n433\t\\label{tab:entropy}\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{lccl}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\t\\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{Entropy at Onset} & \\textbf{Direction} & \\textbf{Pathway} \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\t008d s1 (CIVO) & $-$3.47 & $\\downarrow$ to $-$4.88 & A (value-driven) \\\\\n439\t008e\\_v3 s2 & $-$5.21 & $\\uparrow$ to $-$3.83 & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n440\t008a (healthy) & $-$4.20 & stable $\\pm$0.3 & --- \\\\\n441\tBaseline & $-$5.00 & stable $\\pm$0.2 & --- \\\\\n442\t\\bottomrule\n443\t\\end{tabular}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t\n446\t\\subsection{Pathway A vs.\\ Pathway B: Detailed Comparison}\n447\t\n448\tTable~\\ref{tab:pathways} contrasts the two collapse pathways observed under different imagination strategies with trained dt-embedding.\n449\t\n450\t\\begin{table}[h]\n451\t\\centering\n452\t\\caption{Pathway A (CIVO, dt$=$2 imagination) vs.\\ Pathway B (eval-driven, dt$=$1 imagination). Qualitatively distinct dynamics despite both producing high NVE.}\n453\t\\label{tab:pathways}\n454\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n455\t\\toprule\n456\t\\textbf{Feature} & \\textbf{Pathway A (008d s1)} & \\textbf{Pathway B (008e\\_v3 s2)} \\\\\n457\t\\midrule\n458\tValue change at collapse & +130\\% explosion & +13\\% mild \\\\\n459\tEval change at collapse & $-$43\\% follows value & $-$81\\% leads \\\\\n460\tNVE driver & Value numerator & Eval denominator \\\\\n461\tActor entropy & $\\downarrow$ ($-$3.47 $\\to$ $-$4.88) & $\\uparrow$ ($-$5.21 $\\to$ $-$3.83) \\\\\n462\tMechanism & Critic overestimate $\\to$ actor exploit & Eval crash $\\to$ value lag \\\\\n463\t\\bottomrule\n464\t\\end{tabular}\n465\t\\end{table}\n466\t\n467\t\\subsection{dt$=$1 Imagination as Partial Mitigation (C6)}\n468\t\n469\tRestricting imagination to dt$=$1 while keeping trained dt-embedding and subsample ON (008e\\_v3) eliminates Pathway A (classic CIVO) in all 4 seeds.\n470\tHowever, 1/4 seeds (seed 2) collapses via Pathway B (eval 137@105K, NVE 3.78$\\times$), with gate\\_dt\\_mean$=$1.0 throughout---confirming dt$=$1 imagination was never violated.\n471\t\n472\t\\begin{table}[h]\n473\t\\centering\n474\t\\caption{008e\\_v3 cross-seed summary (dt$=$1 imagination, trained dt-embedding). Pathway A is eliminated; Pathway B affects 1/4 seeds.}\n475\t\\label{tab:mitigation}\n476\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n477\t\\toprule\n478\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Eval 105K} & \\textbf{NVE 105K} & \\textbf{Collapse} & \\textbf{Pathway} \\\\\n479\t\\midrule\n480\t1 & 869.9 & 0.80 & No & --- \\\\\n481\t2 & 137.0 & 3.78 & Yes & B (eval-driven) \\\\\n482\t3 & 840.0 & 0.78 & No & --- \\\\\n483\t4 & 810.0 & 0.82 & No & --- \\\\\n484\t\\bottomrule\n485\t\\end{tabular}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\t\\subsection{Frozen dt-Embedding: Gradient Starvation}\n489\t\n490\tFreezing the dt-embedding (015b: requires\\_grad$=$False, random init) eliminates CIVO (NVE 0.39--0.47) but introduces gradient starvation: actor\\_loss converges to $1.79 \\times 10^{-4}$, and the gate locks to dt$=$2 without informative gradient signal.\n491\tEval reaches only 204@105K.\n492\tThis confirms that dt-embedding \\emph{training} is the necessary condition for CIVO; a frozen random embedding blocks the feedback loop but creates a distinct failure mode.\n493\t\n494\t\\subsection{Uncertainty-Gated Imagination Truncation (Pilot)}\n495\t\n496\tAs a proof-of-concept mitigation, we truncate imagination steps where RSSM prediction uncertainty exceeds a threshold.\n497\tThis reduces the effective horizon from H$=$8 to $\\sim$5.2 (35\\% truncation rate) and keeps NVE below 1.5$\\times$ (peak 1.2$\\times$, no collapse).\n498\tHowever, eval reaches only 580@105K ($-$37\\% vs.\\ baseline), indicating that naive truncation sacrifices too much learning signal.\n499\tThis confirms the causal chain (low-quality predictions $\\to$ value overestimation) and frames future work on smarter truncation policies."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhLTKsURT834Q9J999V9EY","session_id":"1b0edaaf-f3d3-4d02-90e4-a445b4dd87c2","uuid":"891c0913-9d30-4b81-8c56-2ccacb783f5e","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:31:08.038Z"}
